<%#
  Onboarding — the first of the two post-auth flows, and the first thing a
  brand-new account sees. ONE card: the first-name ask.

    first-name  — captured for marketing; SKIPPABLE, and skipping is remembered
                  for the session so it does not re-ask on the next page

  WHAT WAS HERE BEFORE (operator call, 2026-08-15): a `welcome` step opened this
  modal with "You're in" and the auto-generated username, and an internal step
  machine walked welcome → first-name. Both are gone. The welcome card cost a
  click to deliver something the user had not asked for, and with one step left
  the machine — the step getter, the two <template x-if> branches, the
  asksFirstName lookahead — was a switch with a single arm. The card renders
  directly now, and the modal takes NO props.

  The chain does not end here. On completion this dispatches
  'onboarding-step-done' with the steps the SERVER says are still outstanding,
  and the layout's chain driver opens the next flow (Wallet setup: age → wallet).

  TWO CALLERS, ONE CARD:
    - the post-auth chain, which opens it with no props. Skippable — this lands
      seconds after signup, the highest drop-off moment there is.
    - the ENTRY GATE (operator call, 2026-08-15), which opens it with
      { required: true } as the first validation of hold-to-confirm. There the
      skip affordances are hidden: the hold cannot proceed without the name, so
      offering to skip would promise something the gate will not honour. Closing
      is still allowed — it abandons the entry, it does not slip past it.

  On a successful save this ALSO clears $store.session.firstNameRequired and
  dispatches 'first-name-saved', which is how the board resumes an entry that
  was interrupted by the gate — the same shape as the age modal's 'age-verified'.

  Props (all optional):
    required — hide the skip affordances (the entry-gate caller)

  CRITICAL (Alpine): cloned from <template x-if>, so ONE root element; and the
  x-data below is a DOUBLE-QUOTED attribute — a single " anywhere inside it (a
  code comment included) closes it early and the whole modal mounts as a silent
  no-op that still renders markup. Keep every string SINGLE-quoted.
%>
<div x-data="{
       get props() { var c = $store.modals.current(); return (c && c.props) || {}; },
       get required() { return !!this.props.required; },
       firstName: '',
       submitting: false,
       error: '',
       async save() {
         if (this.submitting) return;
         var value = (this.firstName || '').trim();
         if (!value) { this.error = 'Enter your first name, or skip for now.'; return; }
         this.submitting = true; this.error = '';
         var data = await this.post('/onboarding/first_name', { first_name: value });
         this.submitting = false;
         if (!data || !data.ok) {
           this.error = (data && data.error) || 'Could not save that — try again.';
           return;
         }
         // The entry gate reads the STORE, not the server, on the resumed hold —
         // so clear it here or the resumed confirmEntry re-blocks on a name that
         // is already saved. Dispatched before finish(), which closes the card.
         var sess = Alpine.store('session');
         if (sess) sess.firstNameRequired = false;
         window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('first-name-saved'));
         this.finish(data.next || []);
       },
       async skip() {
         if (this.submitting) return;
         this.submitting = true; this.error = '';
         var data = await this.post('/onboarding/skip_first_name', {});
         this.submitting = false;
         this.finish((data && data.next) || []);
       },
       async post(url, body) {
         var meta = document.querySelector('meta[name=csrf-token]');
         try {
           var res = await fetch(url, {
             method: 'POST',
             headers: {
               'Content-Type': 'application/json',
               'Accept': 'application/json',
               'X-CSRF-Token': meta ? meta.content : ''
             },
             body: JSON.stringify(body)
           });
           return await res.json();
         } catch (e) { return null; }
       },
       // Hand the REMAINING steps to the chain driver and get out of the way.
       // Closing here (rather than letting the driver swap) keeps this modal
       // ignorant of what comes next — the driver owns the order.
       finish(next) {
         window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('onboarding-step-done', { detail: { next: next } }));
         $store.modals.close();
       }
     }"
     class="relative">

  <%# === FIRST NAME ========================================================= %>
  <%# Skippable in the CHAIN by design: it lands seconds after signup, the
      highest drop-off moment there is, so the Skip link is a peer of Save rather
      than buried. Skipping is recorded for the session only — the field stays
      blank, so a later visit may ask again.

      At the ENTRY GATE (required) the × merely CLOSES: skipping would record a
      session skip that the gate deliberately ignores (it reads the column), so
      the button would look like a way past a wall it cannot move. %>
  <div class="relative mb-3 -mt-2">
    <h3 class="text-heading font-bold text-lg leading-tight text-center pt-1">What should we call you?</h3>
    <button @click="required ? $store.modals.close() : skip()"
            class="absolute top-0 right-0 -mr-2 text-secondary hover:text-heading text-xl leading-none"
            :aria-label="required ? 'Close' : 'Skip'">&times;</button>
  </div>
  <%# Same 2-of-3 as the wallet step: the app's pill frame is
      sign in (auth modal, 1) → set up (this whole chain, 2) → play (3), so
      every setup step reads 2, not its own index within the chain. %>
  <%= render "studio/modals/blocks/progress_pill", current: 2, total: 3 %>
  <%# Two readings of the same ask. From the chain it is an invitation; from the
      entry gate it is the last thing between the user and their picks, and
      saying so is what makes the missing Skip link read as intent. %>
  <p class="text-xs text-body mb-4 text-center" x-show="!required">
    Just your first name — we use it to address you in emails about your
    contests and payouts.
  </p>
  <p class="text-xs text-body mb-4 text-center" x-show="required" x-cloak>
    One last thing before your entry — just your first name, so we can address
    you in emails about your contests and payouts.
  </p>

  <form @submit.prevent="save()" novalidate>
    <div class="mb-3">
      <label class="block text-xs text-secondary mb-1 font-medium" for="onboarding-first-name">First name</label>
      <%# Autofocus so the user can just type (operator call). The plain HTML
          autofocus attribute is NOT enough here: browsers honour it when the
          document is parsed, and this modal mounts from a <template x-if>
          long after that — so it needs Alpine. $nextTick waits for the step's
          DOM to exist before focusing; preventScroll keeps the mount
          animation from being yanked by the browser scrolling to the field. %>
      <input id="onboarding-first-name" type="text" x-model="firstName"
             maxlength="40" autocomplete="given-name" enterkeyhint="done"
             placeholder="Alex"
             :disabled="submitting"
             x-init="$nextTick(() => $el.focus({ preventScroll: true }))"
             class="input-field">
    </div>
    <template x-if="error">
      <p class="text-red-400 text-xs mb-3" x-text="error"></p>
    </template>
    <button type="submit" :disabled="submitting"
            class="btn btn-primary btn-lg w-full gap-2 disabled:cursor-wait">
      <span x-show="submitting" class="cta-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span x-text="submitting ? 'Saving…' : 'Save and continue'"></span>
    </button>
  </form>

  <%# Hidden at the entry gate — see the × above for why offering it there would
      promise a way past a wall that does not move. %>
  <button type="button" x-show="!required" @click="skip()" :disabled="submitting"
          class="mt-3 w-full text-center text-sm text-secondary hover:text-heading disabled:opacity-50">
    Skip for now
  </button>
</div>
